---
# DID THE SITE STOP KNOWING WHICH FILM A LISTING IS? -- the live half of "something stopped being
# matched / changed match". The code-caused half is CI's (the identity takes check in every
# convergence leg resolves the pinned recording at the base and at the head and fails on a moved
# take nobody accepted); this file watches what production actually did, whatever moved it --
# new data, an answer store that changed, an agreement stage taking or withdrawing a film, or a
# deploy.
#
# THE COUNTER: `kinowo_worker_identity_take_changes_total{country,kind}`, written by every identity
# projection that writes (services.identity.TakeChange): a listing STILL PUBLISHED whose TMDB film
# moved -- `lost` (had one, has none), `changed` (another film), `gained` (had none). Expected churn
# is never counted: a listing leaving the schedule has no cluster, a new one no previous film, and a
# re-key or merge that keeps the TMDB film keeps the take. Each change is also logged one line
# (`identity take change: <kind> <venue> | <listing>: <old> -> <new>; reason <basis: why>`), so the
# drill-down is a VictoriaLogs query on that phrase with `country:<cc>`.
#
# WHY A SHARE OF THE COUNTRY'S LISTINGS AND NOT A COUNT. Countries differ 20x in size (ES 5,178
# listings, US 101,678 on 2026-10-13) and in listings per film (PL 9, US 48): one US wide release
# re-matched is ~500 listings, one PL film ~9. A count either pages on every US re-match or never on
# PL. The share asks the same question everywhere: did a visible slice of what the country serves
# change film within the hour?
groups:
  - name: identity-matching
    rules:
      # 2% OF A COUNTRY'S LISTINGS LOST OR RE-MATCHED WITHIN AN HOUR, held for 15 minutes.
      #
      # THE BASELINE, measured before this counter existed from the projection's own canary
      # (`kinowo_worker_identity_cutover_canary{relation="moved"}`: clusters taking another TMDB
      # film than the film they were on), hourly max over 10 days to 2026-10-13: median 0 in every
      # country, p99 3-7 clusters, max 4 (US) to 111 (PL). In listings that p99 is at most ~1% of a
      # country (PL: 7 clusters x 9 listings = 63 of 9,408 = 0.7%); the bursts above it were the
      # identity cut-over's own pushes. The `> 50` floor keeps Spain's 5,178 listings from paging on
      # two films. The counter's own first 1.5 hours live (2026-10-13 13:25-15:00Z): worst hour DE 8 lost+changed
      # listings (0.02% of 34,436), UK 3, PL/US/ES 0 -- the threshold sits two orders of magnitude above it.
      #
      # `increase(...[1h])` is a range function, so a worker restart's ~3-minute scrape gap leaves
      # fewer samples, not a reset `for:` (the restart case in the test pins it), and the listings
      # gauge is read through `max_over_time` for the same reason.
      - alert: IdentityTakeChangesBurst
        expr: |
          (
            sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_identity_take_changes_total{kind=~"lost|changed"}[1h]))
              /
            max by (country) (max_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_cutover_listings[1h]))
          ) > 0.02
          and
          sum by (country) (increase(kinowo_worker_identity_take_changes_total{kind=~"lost|changed"}[1h])) > 50
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of its listings lost or changed their film within an hour"
          description: >-
            The identity projection moved the TMDB film of more than 2% of this country's published
            listings in the last hour -- lost (matched before, unmatched now) or changed (another
            film). Listings leaving the schedule and re-keys that keep the film are not counted, so
            this is the site showing different films under the same listings. Which ones and why:
            VictoriaLogs `country:<cc> "identity take change"` (one line per listing: venue,
            listing, old -> new film, the resolver's reason). A burst right after a worker deploy is
            the deploy's resolver change -- CI's identity takes check should have listed it; one
            with no deploy is the data or an answer store moving (the agreement stage, a TMDB
            answer re-read). Panel: worker diagnostics, "Identity cutover -- regroupings".

      # MORE THAN 10 SUSPECT TAKES, AND 5%, ABOVE THE SAME TIME YESTERDAY, held for 2 hours.
      #
      # `kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes{signal}` (SuspectTakeCount, f6d8cdac3): the billings whose take a
      # suspect-take signal (fragment, era, runtime, sequel, feed id, fill route) finds against its own evidence,
      # set by a whole-corpus projection whose decisions or listings moved. The level is the corpus's standing
      # doubt; a RISE over a day is new wrong takes landing. The convergence legs' suspect-take ratchet names
      # code-caused ones; this sees the rest (new data, an answer store moving).
      #
      # A GAUGE SET ONLY NOW AND THEN, and absent for ~3 minutes after every worker restart until the next whole
      # projection counts again: both sides read it through `last_over_time(...[3h])`, which bridges both (the
      # restart case in the test pins it), and a country with no value a day ago compares against nothing and
      # stays quiet.
      #
      # THE LEVELS, live on 2026-10-13 15:00Z (the gauge's first hours): DE 222, UK 101, PL 106, US 13, ES 2. The
      # proposed +10 alone is 4.5% of Germany's standing count, inside what a week's new programmes can add with no
      # wrong take among them; the 5% beside it keeps the +10 floor for the small countries and asks DE/UK for a
      # rise their size makes notable. Re-check against a week of the gauge's own day-over-day spread.
      - alert: IdentitySuspectTakesRising
        expr: |
          (
            sum by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes[3h]))
              -
            sum by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes[3h] offset 1d))
          ) > 10
          and
          (
            sum by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes[3h]))
              /
            sum by (country) (last_over_time(kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes[3h] offset 1d))
          ) > 1.05
        for: 2h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value }} more suspect takes than a day ago"
          description: >-
            The live resolve's suspect takes (billings whose film a signal doubts against the listing's own
            evidence: a title fragment, an era, a runtime, a sequel number, a feed id, a fill route) rose by more
            than 10 over the same time yesterday, for two hours. Which signal: worker diagnostics, the identity
            cutover panel (`kinowo_worker_identity_suspect_takes` by signal); which takes: the suspect-take
            audit (scripts.SuspectTakesMeasure over a recording, or the convergence legs' step summary on the next push). A rise
            right after a worker deploy is the deploy's; IdentityTakeChangesBurst firing beside it says the films
            themselves moved.
